WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.500
The extra leg enlarged the target.

2
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:07.760
Suppose it doubled the support.

3
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.040
Could the survivors double too?

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.440
Twice 12,000 is 24,000.

5
00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.540
But only about 20,000 were present at the peak.

6
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.930
If no new neurons join afterwards, no target can rescue more than that.

7
00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:29.670
That is the ceiling.

8
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.860
And the real rise was well below it.

9
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:38.320
In embryos with vigorously moving grafts, survivors rose by a tenth to over a

10
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.320
quarter.

11
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:43.533
So the target turns a dial.

12
00:00:44.393 --> 00:00:46.593
More target, fewer deaths, up to the ceiling.

13
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:49.147
Why did it turn so little?

14
00:00:50.007 --> 00:00:51.367
The experiment cannot tell.

15
00:00:52.207 --> 00:00:54.167
The graft may be only partly innervated.

16
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.400
Activity at the muscle also matters.

17
00:00:58.433 --> 00:01:04.033
In chick embryos, paralysing the muscle with curare rescues many motor neurons that

18
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:05.033
would otherwise die.

19
00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:08.603
Is every developmental death the target's doing?

20
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:11.483
One test adds cells instead of target.

21
00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:17.643
Derek Southwell and colleagues transplanted precursors of cortical interneurons into

22
00:01:17.643 --> 00:01:21.063
host mice, across a 200-fold range of doses.

23
00:01:22.183 --> 00:01:28.143
Predict. Take a toy model, where the host can support only 1,000 transplanted cells.

24
00:01:29.023 --> 00:01:30.923
What fraction dies as the dose rises?

25
00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:40.957
In the toy model, none die until the dose passes 1,000.

26
00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:43.957
Then the fraction climbs, to 95%.

27
00:01:43.957 --> 00:01:44.957
percent.

28
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:47.067
The real result was different.

29
00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:50.587
About the same fraction died at every dose tested.

30
00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.190
the cells died at their own age, not the host's.

31
00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.210
They carried their own clock.

32
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.680
Later work showed that, during this window, circuit activity can tune how many

33
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:02.680
survive.

34
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.220
The chick has another case, in the neck-level, or cervical, cord.

35
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:16.010
Around day 4 to 5, a wave of cervical motor neurons dies, before the later death

36
00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.470
that follows target contact.

37
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.320
A bigger target did not change this early wave.

38
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.880
Nor did paralysis, or the trophic factors tested.

39
00:02:25.883 --> 00:02:29.503
Drugs that block RNA or protein synthesis reduced it.

40
00:02:30.243 --> 00:02:33.303
So it needs new RNA and protein, an active program.

41
00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:39.077
Moved to another level of the cord, the cervical tube still lost its neurons on

42
00:02:39.077 --> 00:02:40.077
schedule.

43
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.340
So, make too many, then let the target keep what it can support.

44
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:51.590
That holds for many peripheral neurons, and for motor neurons at their muscles.

45
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.400
Takeaway: in the limb, the target helps set survival, like a dial with a ceiling.

46
00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.180
Count across time, and you can see it.
